From f4996fccc2c9a449aa787280b6ed9cf1feb0f931 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tomaz Zaman <tomaz@mono.si>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 02:09:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] netfilter: walk the forwarding path through sit and ip6_tnl
 devices

A flow whose egress is an IP-in-IP tunnel stopped at the tunnel device:
sit and ip6_tnl had no ndo_fill_forward_path, so the walk that lets a
hardware adapter describe VLAN, bridge and PPPoE paths never reached
the port the outer packet leaves by, and the reverse direction kept the
tunnel device as its ingress, where no hardware can match it.

Give both drivers the hook. Each resolves the hop exactly as its
transmit path would -- the outer route, its next hop and that hop's
neighbour on the device below -- and records the outer header it would
prepend in a new DEV_PATH_TUNNEL entry: family, protocol, TTL, TOS or
traffic class, flow label, the endpoints, the next hop and the per-tunnel
properties software decides once (inherited TOS, DF, the encapsulation
limit option, DSCP copy on receive). The walk then continues on the
outer route's device with ctx->daddr set to the next hop's address, so a
tunnel over a VLAN device, a bridge or a PPPoE session is descended like
any upper device. Only a point-to-point tunnel is a hop: 6rd, ISATAP,
NBMA, collect_md, FOU/GUE, a per-packet mark and a transformed outer
route are refused by the hook.

The flowtable carries the hop whole, as it carries a PPPoE session:
nf_flow_tunnel on the route and the tuple, handed to the driver as
nf_tunnel and nf_tunnel_reverse beside the two destinations. Nothing
else describes it -- no push action for the header a direction inserts,
no pop or dissector key for the one it strips -- and it spends no
encapsulation slot, since those are the L2 headers a rule can pop and
push. Only a table that asked for neighbour output takes the hop; any
other keeps the tunnel device as the reverse ingress, where the software
path matches the decapsulated packet, which moving the ingress to the
port below would lose.

Context version 9.
